WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.692
Validation logic should be tested as deliberately as business logic.

00:00:04.692 --> 00:00:10.200
Unit tests should confirm that valid inputs pass and invalid inputs fail.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.709
Negative tests are especially important because validation exists to reject bad data.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:24.481
Test missing fields, wrong types, invalid formats, excessive lengths, boundary values, unexpected nested structures, and values that violate business rules.

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:27.541
Boundary tests catch many real defects.

00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:35.497
If a field allows one to one hundred items, test zero, one, one hundred, and one hundred one.

00:00:35.497 --> 00:00:42.230
If a number has a maximum, test the maximum and the value just above it.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:48.962
If a string has a length limit, test empty strings, maximum length, and overlong input.

00:00:48.962 --> 00:00:52.022
These tests make the contract concrete.

00:00:52.022 --> 00:01:00.386
Property-based testing and fuzzing, at a high level, can help discover cases developers did not think to write manually.

00:01:00.386 --> 00:01:09.159
They are not replacements for clear requirements, but they can expose parser issues, encoding surprises, type confusion, and boundary failures.

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:15.483
Use them where input parsing or transformation is complex enough to justify broader exploration.

00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:18.543
Invalid input should be logged safely.

00:01:18.543 --> 00:01:30.579
Logs can help detect abuse and debug integration problems, but raw invalid input may contain secrets, personal data, control characters, or payloads that make logs hard to read.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:38.128
Log enough context to support troubleshooting and monitoring, but avoid storing sensitive or dangerous raw values unnecessarily.

00:01:38.128 --> 00:01:43.228
Testing should cover behavior, errors, and observability, not only happy paths.
